  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "None noted; the full paper text, including appendices, is present.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "Applies the model-capacity idea in a new setting, SAS-ATR, and reports held-out performance for different capacity variants. It does not measure a complete double-descent curve.",
    "gate_note": "The paper evaluates capacity variants using held-out Average Precision (AP) and Optimal F1, and reports their outcomes. It does not sweep through an interpolation threshold or report test error as a function of size, epochs, or sample count. The low-capacity TinyCNN and ResNet18 comparisons are not included as capacity-sweep rows because they are different architectures rather than variants in those sweeps.",
    "test_reason": "The paper adopts double descent as a design principle and compares discrete capacity variants (22M–303M parameters) on SAS-ATR. It states that every Table 1 model reached near-perfect training fit (loss ≤1e-3). So all variants sit past the interpolation threshold, and no sweep crosses it. There is no test-error curve over size, epochs or samples. No peak, second descent, noise effect or effective-complexity measure is measured. Each variant's regularization is tuned separately. The results show the study's own models performing well rather than testing any version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors conclude that increasing capacity usually improves SAS-ATR performance, often substantially, and is never significantly detrimental. They also conclude that regularization is crucial to realize these gains, and that properly regularized high-capacity DNNs are promising for SAS-ATR. They do not claim to have observed double descent; they use it as motivation.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study does not reproduce a double-descent experiment: it compares discrete capacity variants on SAS-ATR using held-out AP and Optimal F1, without locating an interpolation threshold or measuring a full risk curve.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported capacity gains vary by architecture; the paper describes more modest gains or slight degradation for some comparisons. Regularization is separately optimized for the reported best-performing models.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Among models that all interpolate the training set, larger same-family variants usually score higher AP and Opt. F1 (e.g. ResNeXt50→152 AP 79.94→89.54). Some pairs are flat or slightly worse (ResNet101→152 AP, ViT Base→Large).",
        "proposed_claim": "Within the overparameterized regime on a small, domain-specific dataset, increasing capacity (with tuned regularization) usually improves held-out performance, but not monotonically.",
